7fb0459e-9095-437b-b568-1f81cc6b9fa9	01_glass_transition_thermal_properties	textbook	en	The viscosity of the glass at Tg is typically in the range of 10^12 to 10^13 Pa.s, corresponding to the point where the relaxation time equals the experimental observation time. For soda-lime-silica glass, which is the most widely produced commercial glass, Tg is approximately 550 to 580 degrees Celsius. For borosilicate glass (e.g., Pyrex), Tg is higher, around 525 degrees Celsius. Fused silica, the purest form of silica glass, has a Tg of approximately 1200 degrees Celsius due to the strong Si-O network. 1.1 Factors Affecting Tg The glass transition temperature is strongly influenced by the chemical composition of the glass. Network modifiers such as Na2O and K2O lower Tg by breaking up the silicate network and reducing the average connectivity. Network formers such as SiO2, B2O3, and Al2O3 generally increase Tg by strengthening the network. The cooling rate also has a significant effect on the observed Tg. A faster cooling rate results in a higher fictive temperature and a higher apparent Tg, because the glass structure is frozen in at a higher temperature.	{"file_path": "C:\\\\Users\\\\Documents\\\\Projects\\\\glass-expert-ai-phase1\\\\glass-expert-ai\\\\data\\\\sample_docs\\\\01_glass_transition_thermal_properties.pdf", "page_count": 4, "chunk_index": 1, "ingested_at": "2026-03-04T13:49:03.442329", "total_chunks": 8}	[-0.006278146,0.053023346,-0.0059470846,0.05918537,-0.051606093,-0.015723642,-0.017550558,-0.00083880976,-0.030497773,0.04445622,-0.019075725,0.021364504,0.020960372,-0.07710279,-0.016179917,0.0152871,-0.010331314,0.024622167,-0.028318254,-0.0019295533,0.021875381,0.04205929,-0.05737063,-0.045716453,-0.065112606,0.023989052,-0.0187608,-0.034046352,0.07672494,0.047919977,-0.0028602704,0.010392333,-0.0016463668,-0.0405705,0.015645396,-0.0062917843,0.03804111,-0.016184993,-0.017183468,-0.051752828,0.049197737,-0.02020182,0.020480478,0.0032423118,-0.014871743,-0.032