7e8ded1c-2e09-487c-9907-461ebff456ca	01_glass_transition_thermal_properties	textbook	en	It controls the melting, fining, forming, and annealing processes. The viscosity of glass melts varies over many orders of magnitude with temperature, from approximately 10^1 Pa.s at melting temperatures to 10^12 Pa.s at the annealing point. The Vogel-Fulcher-Tammann (VFT) equation is the most widely used model to describe the temperature dependence of glass melt viscosity: log(eta) = A + B / (T - T0) Where eta is the dynamic viscosity (Pa.s), T is the temperature in Celsius, and A, B, T0 are empirical constants specific to each glass composition. The parameter T0 is often interpreted as the ideal glass transition temperature, below which the viscosity would theoretically become infinite. For soda-lime-silica glass, typical VFT parameters are: A = -2.485, B = 4545, T0 = 268 (temperatures in Celsius).	{"file_path": "C:\\\\Users\\\\Documents\\\\Projects\\\\glass-expert-ai-phase1\\\\glass-expert-ai\\\\data\\\\sample_docs\\\\01_glass_transition_thermal_properties.pdf", "page_count": 4, "chunk_index": 4, "ingested_at": "2026-03-04T13:49:03.581248", "total_chunks": 8}	[0.0024946847,0.04790233,0.018704714,0.051583514,-0.062474817,-0.00072278245,-0.022227375,0.0007471012,-0.02183337,0.05957066,-0.01122432,0.016708352,0.011457934,-0.068665385,-0.003271106,0.036565293,0.0061488105,0.009406387,-0.05861054,0.019433055,-0.01185396,0.045887392,-0.042219475,-0.051977135,-0.012419389,0.007849854,0.009184756,-0.05021542,0.09335266,0.029498678,-0.0056313844,-0.003989221,0.04474363,-0.051037624,0.00889081,0.037678555,0.026540162,-0.03281178,-0.016987525,-0.05207195,0.064939685,0.008795708,0.028754735,0.014404226,-0.019118398,-0.00087968766,0.022735462,-0.049919445,-0.047262505,-0.06275444,-0.002867299,0.0039116153,0.01180618,-0.04170141,-0.0050136168,0.04187489,-0.01621223,0.013744546,-0.018191164,0.046960585,0.010353332,0.015214822,0.018834345,-0.03283622,0.005257118,0.01976749,0.0072065718,0.0252