3eccd089-efd6-48cb-b397-efeb08e253b3	01_glass_transition_thermal_properties	textbook	en	For glasses, the CTE is typically expressed in units of 10^-7 /K (or ppm/K) and is measured over a defined temperature range, usually 20 to 300 degrees Celsius. The CTE of glass is critically important in applications where the glass must be sealed to metals or ceramics, where thermal shock resistance is required, or where dimensional stability over a wide temperature range is needed. Mismatches in CTE between glass and adjacent materials can lead to cracking or seal failure. The CTE of silicate glasses can be estimated using the Appen additive model, which calculates the CTE as a weighted sum of the contributions of each oxide component: CTE = sum(xi * CTEi) Where xi is the mole fraction of oxide i and CTEi is the CTE contribution of that oxide. Na2O has a large positive contribution (~33 x 10^-7 /K per mole fraction), while SiO2 has a small positive contribution (~3.8 x 10^-7 /K per mole fraction).	{"file_path": "C:\\\\Users\\\\Documents\\\\Projects\\\\glass-expert-ai-phase1\\\\glass-expert-ai\\\\data\\\\sample_docs\\\\01_glass_transition_thermal_properties.pdf", "page_count": 4, "chunk_index": 6, "ingested_at": "2026-03-04T13:49:03.669321", "total_chunks": 8}	[-0.0207444,0.02262741,0.007312258,0.030063597,-0.08286496,-0.0059435284,-0.03166737,0.0074192765,-0.016265713,0.06745099,0.022827348,0.029399546,0.0009031773,-0.04090026,-0.034733046,0.02923882,-0.019290311,0.008805876,-0.023387155,0.007962092,0.026954649,0.039860573,-0.09466603,-0.037754595,-0.0425657,-0.0032297394,0.011380904,-0.04069041,0.095846616,0.02691976,0.007561631,-0.021659153,-0.02361904,-0.06143649,-0.006303399,-0.01991956,0.047385868,0.015619159,-0.011875671,-0.042753953,0.041911043,-0.007982512,0.032274388,-0.024511255,-0.02047344,-0.036007185,-0.014682312,-0.07745,-0.0026266468,-0.028346116,-0.0019871974,0.012312501,0.0217917,-0.067572266,0.045917217,0.033924926,0.018499412,0.012978708,-0.041526444,-0.0090