89fd6d44-354e-41bf-ab94-7f8c31384e9c	02_glass_composition_properties	textbook	en	It is a critical property for glass used in pharmaceutical packaging, laboratory equipment, food containers, and architectural applications. The primary mechanism of glass corrosion in aqueous environments involves two processes: ion exchange (leaching) and network dissolution. In the leaching process, alkali ions (Na+, K+) in the glass exchange with H+ or H3O+ ions from the solution, leaving a silica-rich leached layer on the glass surface. In network dissolution, the silicate network itself is attacked, particularly at high pH where OH- ions break Si-O-Si bonds. The chemical durability of glass is strongly influenced by composition. High SiO2 content generally improves durability. Al2O3 significantly improves hydrolytic resistance by strengthening the network. CaO and MgO improve durability compared to Na2O and K2O. ZrO2 is particularly effective at improving alkali resistance. Glass durability is classified according to international standards such as ISO 719 (hydrolytic resistance), ISO 695 (alkali resistance), and ISO 1776 (acid resistance).	{"file_path": "C:\\\\Users\\\\Documents\\\\Projects\\\\glass-expert-ai-phase1\\\\glass-expert-ai\\\\data\\\\sample_docs\\\\02_glass_composition_properties.pdf", "page_count": 3, "chunk_index": 6, "ingested_at": "2026-03-04T13:49:30.280656", "total_chunks": 8}	[-0.029245647,0.029819747,-0.002533934,0.027540574,-0.08540811,0.04510671,0.0022737144,0.0044543296,0.036802508,0.06304378,-0.0046313102,0.0045397133,0.050099537,-0.019613048,-0.030336838,0.021566853,-0.020664336,0.041337553,-0.024907326,0.010921051,0.041017212,0.042704172,-0.041732617,-0.025281606,-0.06627936,0.022096545,-0.03369347,-0.052383743,0.04458527,0.04816889,-0.028474614,0.012145819,-0.026055008,-0.034083873,-0.006674228,-0.02184163,0.018894946,-0.047203034,-0.0038018783,-0.05411678,0.060069256,-0.010510965,0.0036591142,0.014375133,-0.013602871,0.014793294,0.016208153,-0.06100937