20911ee6-5e69-4534-a28b-d63d807d5389	03_glass_defects_troubleshooting_sop	sop	en	Glass Manufacturing Defects -- Identification & Troubleshooting SOP Glass Manufacturing Defects Identification and Troubleshooting SOP Glass Expert AI -- Sample SOP Document | Internal Reference Section 1: Stones and Crystalline Inclusions Stones are solid crystalline or amorphous inclusions in glass that did not dissolve during the melting process. They are one of the most serious defects in glass manufacturing because they can cause stress concentrations that lead to spontaneous breakage. IDENTIFICATION: Stones appear as opaque or semi-opaque particles, typically white, grey, or colored, embedded within the glass. They can be identified by their sharp boundaries with the surrounding glass, their crystalline appearance under polarized light, and their chemical composition as determined by EDS or XRF analysis. COMMON TYPES AND CAUSES: 1. Silica stones (cristobalite/quartz): Undissolved sand grains. Caused by insufficient melting temperature, too-coarse sand particle size, or insufficient melting time. 2. Refractory stones: Fragments from furnace refractory materials. Caused by refractory corrosion, thermal shock to refractory, or mechanical damage. 3. Devitrification stones: Crystalline phases that precipitate from the glass melt.	{"file_path": "C:\\\\Users\\\\Documents\\\\Projects\\\\glass-expert-ai-phase1\\\\glass-expert-ai\\\\data\\\\sample_docs\\\\03_glass_defects_troubleshooting_sop.pdf", "page_count": 4, "chunk_index": 0, "ingested_at": "2026-03-04T13:49:48.660148", "total_chunks": 10}	[-0.051441558,0.0030001695,0.007055661,0.04755052,-0.064010896,-0.009012329,-0.028982578,-0.008218782,0.024691457,0.032651465,-0.0025095516,0.040812828,0.022257704,-0.04623839,-0.0423276,0.013758714,-0.032376,0.020609448,-0.032035157,0.021336298,0.022062812,0.037244406,-0.027317554,-0.03216628,0.009584723,0.024537975,-0.05032708,-0.0009752771,0.075863324,0.00747201,-0.07136415,-0.009584879,0.0354189