81ac5508-f6d4-47ac-a1fe-d8b5e877e38a	03_glass_defects_troubleshooting_sop	sop	en	Refractory stones: Fragments from furnace refractory materials. Caused by refractory corrosion, thermal shock to refractory, or mechanical damage. 3. Devitrification stones: Crystalline phases that precipitate from the glass melt. Caused by contamination, incorrect composition, or slow cooling through the devitrification temperature range. 4. Batch stones: Incompletely reacted batch materials. Caused by poor batch mixing, incorrect batch composition, or insufficient melting. CORRECTIVE ACTIONS: - Increase melting temperature by 20-30 degrees C - Extend melting time - Check and correct batch composition and mixing - Inspect and replace damaged refractory sections - Review cullet quality and contamination sources Glass Expert AI -- Sample Document | Page 1 Glass Manufacturing Defects -- Identification & Troubleshooting SOP Section 2: Bubbles and Seeds Bubbles (also called blisters or seeds when very small) are gas-filled voids in the glass. They range in size from less than 0.1 mm (seeds) to several millimeters (blisters). Bubbles are a common defect in glass manufacturing and can significantly impair optical and mechanical properties. IDENTIFICATION: Bubbles appear as spherical or elongated voids.	{"file_path": "C:\\\\Users\\\\Documents\\\\Projects\\\\glass-expert-ai-phase1\\\\glass-expert-ai\\\\data\\\\sample_docs\\\\03_glass_defects_troubleshooting_sop.pdf", "page_count": 4, "chunk_index": 1, "ingested_at": "2026-03-04T13:49:48.663872", "total_chunks": 10}	[-0.03866903,-0.0013507609,0.020684572,0.039333124,-0.054983627,-0.013576971,-0.0042340183,-0.025220023,0.013835364,0.0405662,-0.009174769,0.007927456,0.05269091,-0.05141808,-0.054157812,-0.018120645,-0.022708178,-0.0052540097,-0.044562064,-0.0036976924,0.015770474,0.034186583,-0.018484956,-0.01672682,0.010135281,0.016431667,-0.017466022,0.022551892,0.08095432,0.011526996,-0.050762203,-0.010779575,0.04300516,-0.051523425,-0.013325753