e3950d45-3798-4a6e-b895-cb868c2254f1	03_glass_defects_troubleshooting_sop	sop	en	They range in size from less than 0.1 mm (seeds) to several millimeters (blisters). Bubbles are a common defect in glass manufacturing and can significantly impair optical and mechanical properties. IDENTIFICATION: Bubbles appear as spherical or elongated voids. Seeds are very small bubbles, typically less than 0.5 mm in diameter. Blisters are larger bubbles, often found near the glass surface. The gas composition inside bubbles can be analyzed by mass spectrometry to determine their origin. COMMON CAUSES AND GAS COMPOSITIONS: 1. SO2/SO3 bubbles: From decomposition of sulfate fining agents at high temperatures. Most common in soda-lime glass. Indicates insufficient fining. 2. CO2 bubbles: From incomplete decomposition of carbonate batch materials (limestone, soda ash). Indicates insufficient melting temperature or time. 3. O2 bubbles: From reboil of dissolved oxygen at forming temperatures. Common when glass is reheated after initial fining. 4. N2 bubbles: From dissolved nitrogen in the melt, often from combustion atmosphere. 5. Water vapor bubbles: From moisture in batch materials or combustion gases.	{"file_path": "C:\\\\Users\\\\Documents\\\\Projects\\\\glass-expert-ai-phase1\\\\glass-expert-ai\\\\data\\\\sample_docs\\\\03_glass_defects_troubleshooting_sop.pdf", "page_count": 4, "chunk_index": 2, "ingested_at": "2026-03-04T13:49:48.711240", "total_chunks": 10}	[-0.021949455,0.011280172,0.03437989,0.003775777,-0.045217715,-0.028211493,0.011614364,-0.009476868,0.03971001,0.036088362,0.012063523,-0.024944013,0.011816873,-0.03565217,-0.043496672,-0.02380413,-0.0025226832,0.025378307,-0.05046646,-0.014918354,0.01870848,0.0094356835,-0.049051818,-0.009417251,-0.0016380385,0.013097164,-0.01615889,0.013359398,0.07528816,0.00096826814,-0.042408235,-0.033827804,0.03574538,-0.04916123,-0.015967999,-0.0015210843,0.04329662,-0.03025563,-0.017166741,-0.037411682,0.08488912,0.03006709,0.035502505,-