06981abc-82c2-4154-80f9-6863c161b920	03_glass_defects_troubleshooting_sop	sop	en	It is a major quality problem in glass manufacturing because crystalline regions have different optical, mechanical, and thermal properties than the surrounding glass, leading to stress concentrations and potential breakage. MECHANISM: Devitrification occurs in two stages: nucleation (formation of crystal nuclei) and crystal growth. The rate of both processes depends strongly on temperature. Nucleation is fastest at temperatures somewhat below Tg, while crystal growth is fastest at higher temperatures. The overall devitrification rate is the product of both processes and shows a maximum at an intermediate temperature. For soda-lime-silica glass, the primary devitrification phase is devitrite (Na2O.3CaO.6SiO2), which forms as needle-like crystals typically at temperatures between 750 and 900 degrees Celsius. Cristobalite (SiO2) can also form at higher temperatures in silica-rich compositions. PREVENTION METHODS: 1. Composition control: Maintain composition within the stable glass-forming region. Avoid compositions near eutectic points in the phase diagram. 2.	{"file_path": "C:\\\\Users\\\\Documents\\\\Projects\\\\glass-expert-ai-phase1\\\\glass-expert-ai\\\\data\\\\sample_docs\\\\03_glass_defects_troubleshooting_sop.pdf", "page_count": 4, "chunk_index": 6, "ingested_at": "2026-03-04T13:49:48.896372", "total_chunks": 10}	[-0.031226689,0.033411793,0.027562555,0.053984277,-0.029938582,-0.010295788,-0.029863214,-0.01567773,-0.002924449,0.0678985,-0.022435883,0.0075231595,0.05639232,-0.05521892,-0.020182882,0.011748883,0.004866385,0.026613504,-0.05183759,0.033108152,0.023207318,0.02294007,-0.020505521,-0.039992362,-0.04386819,0.012096627,-0.014565083,-0.008692,0.090095386,0.042176846,-0.05205139,-0.0058074463,0.02141068,-0.034653712,-0.023790592,0.011311803,0.02544568,-0.004673879,0.011907168,-0.043634627,0.03967415,0.01606561,0.036036044,-0.0003826507,-0.022500642,-0.03654585,-0.041489378,-0